//? Title: Conway's Doomsday Rule: Anchors, Leap Years and Checked Calendar Arithmetic
//? Subtitle: A tiny Christmas calculation, all twelve anchors, complete Gregorian cycles and monthly airline dates
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a year, month and day, we want its weekday without counting forward
through every preceding date. Leap days and century exceptions make a
fixed day-per-year offset insufficient. Conway's Doomsday rule reduces the
calculation to one weekday per year and a remembered reference date in
each month. We work through Christmas 2025, show all twelve common/leap
anchors, retain the original event dates and 2000–2039 figure, and check a
complete 400-year cycle. A real monthly airline dataset demonstrates
calendar annotation without inferring daily traffic from monthly totals.
The canonical API exposes the actual arithmetic, validates dates and
handles negative and extreme int years using explicit Gregorian conventions.
)

\toc

> Start with Christmas 2025

Use weekday codes Sunday=0, Monday=1, …, Saturday=6. The century
containing 2025 begins at 2000 and has anchor Tuesday (2). The year
within the century is 25, with six completed four-year groups. Its
doomsday is (2+25+6) mod 7=5, Friday. December 12 is a remembered
doomsday date, so December 25 lies thirteen days later:

\[
\operatorname{weekday}(2025,12,25)=(5+(25-12))\bmod7=4,
\]

which names Thursday. The owned trace below comes from the same kernel
used by the original weekday function; the report does not reimplement
its century, year or month arithmetic.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/doomsday_christmas.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::doomsday_date_trace(2025,12,25);
  const auto& y=t.year;
  std::print("Century {}; anchor {} ({}); within-century year {}; quarters {}.\n",y.century,y.century_anchor,dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(y.century_anchor),y.year_in_century,y.leap_quarters);
  std::print("Doomsday {} ({}); reference December {}; signed offset {}; final {} ({}).\n",y.doomsday,dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(y.doomsday),t.reference_day,t.offset,t.weekday,dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(t.weekday));
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(78,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;
  box.width=3.9; box.height=1.1; box.font_size=23;
  d.node("century",{0,2},std::format("Century {}\nanchor {} = {}",y.century,y.century_anchor,dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(y.century_anchor)),box)
   .node("year",{5,2},std::format("Year within century {}\ncompleted quarters {}",y.year_in_century,y.leap_quarters),box)
   .node("doomsday",{2.5,0},std::format("({}+{}+{}) mod 7 = {}\n{}",y.century_anchor,y.year_in_century,y.leap_quarters,y.doomsday,dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(y.doomsday)),box)
   .node("date",{2.5,-2.2},std::format("({}+{}) mod 7 = {}\n{}",y.doomsday,t.offset,t.weekday,dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(t.weekday)),box);
  d.edge("century","doomsday").edge("year","doomsday")
   .edge("doomsday","date",std::format("December {} − {} = {}",t.day,t.reference_day,t.offset))
   .text({2.5,3.25},"Actual arithmetic for 2025-12-25",26)
   .save_svg("build/doomsday_christmas.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Century 20; anchor 2 (Tuesday); within-century year 25; quarters 6.
Doomsday 5 (Friday); reference December 12; signed offset 13; final 4 (Thursday).
\image(figures/doomsday_christmas.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Christmas 2025 follows directly from century anchor Tuesday, within-century offset 25+6, annual doomsday Friday and December offset thirteen.)
\alttext(Two upper boxes supply century and year terms to Friday's annual anchor; a thirteen-day offset leads to Thursday.)

> Derive the rule and its mental-arithmetic shortcut

Write any astronomical integer year as y=100c+r with
c=floor(y/100) and 0≤r<100. Floor division matters for negative years;
C++ division alone truncates toward zero. Let mod mean a nonnegative
remainder. The century and year anchors are

\[
A(c)=(5(c\bmod4)+2)\bmod7,\qquad
D(y)=(A(c)+r+\lfloor r/4\rfloor)\bmod7.
\]

D(y) is the weekday of February's last day. Each ordinary year moves
this anchor one weekday forward; entering a leap year moves it two.
The Gregorian leap rule keeps years divisible by four, except centuries
not divisible by 400. A century boundary ending in a non-leap century
therefore advances the anchor by 100+24=124≡5 weekdays; the boundary
ending in a century divisible by 400 advances it by 125≡6.
Starting from year 2000's Tuesday, these give century anchors
Tuesday, Sunday, Friday, Wednesday as c mod 4 runs through 0,1,2,3.

For the mental shortcut, write r=12q+s with 0≤s<12. Then

\[
r+\lfloor r/4\rfloor=15q+s+\lfloor s/4\rfloor
\equiv q+s+\lfloor s/4\rfloor\pmod7.
\]

The trace retains q,s and floor(s/4) as well as the direct terms. These
are two forms of the same arithmetic identity. Finally, choose a valid
reference date a for the requested month and compute
(D(y)+day−a) mod 7. A negative offset is allowed: January 1,2025 lies
two days before January 3, so its weekday is 5−2=3, Wednesday.
Each query uses constant work and fixed storage, independent of the
number of years since an epoch.

> Why all twelve anchors share one weekday

Use January 3 in common years and January 4 in leap years; February's
last day; March 14; then April 4, May 9, June 6, July 11, August 8,
September 5, October 10, November 7 and December 12. The original
report checked eight of these; the next table checks the full twelve
for both 2024 and 2025. March 14 is a week after March 7 and two weeks
after the notional March 0, so these familiar conventions are congruent.
Every public API here requires real valid dates rather than March 0.

Subtract February's last ordinal day from each anchor's ordinal day.
All twelve differences are multiples of seven. In leap years, the
January anchor and all anchors from February onwards shift together
by one ordinal day, keeping the relative week offsets identical.
This establishes the monthly references without assuming their weekdays.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/doomsday_anchors.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto leap=dm::algorithms::doomsday_month_anchors(2024);
  const auto common=dm::algorithms::doomsday_month_anchors(2025);
  std::vector<double> month,weeks;
  std::print("| month | 2024 day | 2024 ordinal | 2025 day | 2025 ordinal | weeks from February end |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<12;++i) {
    const int a=leap[i].day_of_year-leap[1].day_of_year;
    const int b=common[i].day_of_year-common[1].day_of_year;
    if(a!=b || a%7 || leap[i].weekday!=4 || common[i].weekday!=5)
      throw std::runtime_error("monthly anchor mismatch");
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i+1,leap[i].day,leap[i].day_of_year,common[i].day,common[i].day_of_year,b/7);
    month.push_back(i+1); weeks.push_back(b/7);
  }
  std::print("\nAll 24 anchors agree: 2024 Thursday; 2025 Friday.\n");
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.line(month,weeks,"both years",{37,99,235}).points(month,weeks,"",{37,99,235},6)
   .x_ticks(month,{"Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"})
   .title("Every monthly reference is a whole number of weeks from February's end")
   .x_label("month").y_label("signed weeks from February's last day")
   .y_limits(-10,55).size(1100,570).save_svg("build/doomsday_anchors.svg");
}
```
