> Check the complete 400-year cycle and century exceptions

Among 400 consecutive Gregorian years, 100 are divisible by four,
four are centuries, and one is divisible by 400. Hence there are
100−4+1=97 leap years, giving

\[
400\cdot365+97=146097=20871\cdot7
\]

days. The leap pattern and weekdays therefore repeat every 400 years.
This is an exact combinatorial period, not a claim that annual Doomsdays
are uniformly distributed across the seven weekdays.

The next executed scan checks all 146,097 dates in 2000–2399 against
a sequential weekday counter anchored at Saturday,2000-01-01. It also
checks Zeller with the explicit code conversion and compares each date
to the date 2,400 years earlier. Native tests independently supply month
lengths and repeat the daily oracle over five complete cycles, including
negative years. The century controls below show why a simple “every
fourth year” rule fails at 1900 and 2100 but succeeds at 2000 and 2400.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/doomsday_cycle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::array<int,7> counts{};
  int expected=6,days=0,mismatches=0,leaps=0;
  for(int y=2000;y<2400;++y) {
    ++counts[dm::algorithms::doomsday_of_year(y)];
    leaps+=dm::algorithms::doomsday_is_leap(y);
    for(int m=1;m<=12;++m) for(int day=1;day<=dm::algorithms::gregorian_month_days(y,m);++day) {
      const int d=dm::algorithms::doomsday_weekday(y,m,day);
      mismatches+=(d!=expected || d!=(dm::algorithms::zeller_gregorian(y,m,day)+6)%7 || d!=dm::algorithms::doomsday_weekday(y-2400,m,day));
      expected=(expected+1)%7; ++days;
    }
  }
  std::print("Complete cycle: {} years; {} leap years; {} days; {} whole weeks; {} mismatches.\n\n",400,leaps,days,days/7,mismatches);
  if(days!=146097 || leaps!=97 || mismatches || expected!=6) throw std::runtime_error("cycle check failed");
  std::vector<double> count;
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  std::print("| Doomsday | annual count / 400 |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(int w=0;w<7;++w) {
    names.push_back(dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(w).substr(0,3));
    count.push_back(counts[w]);
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(w),counts[w]);
  }
  std::vector<double> advance;
  std::vector<std::string> centuries;
  std::print("\n| entering year | leap | February days | anchor advance modulo 7 |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int y:{1900,2000,2100,2400}) {
    const int shift=(dm::algorithms::doomsday_of_year(y)-dm::algorithms::doomsday_of_year(y-1)+7)%7;
    advance.push_back(shift); centuries.push_back(std::to_string(y));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",y,dm::algorithms::doomsday_is_leap(y),dm::algorithms::gregorian_month_days(y,2),shift);
  }
  auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(count,names,"years",{37,99,235});
  a.title("Annual anchor counts over 400 years").x_label("weekday").y_label("years with this Doomsday").y_limits(0,75);
  auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(advance,centuries,"shift",{217,119,6});
  b.title("Entering a century year").x_label("year").y_label("annual anchor advance (days)").y_limits(0,3);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,550)
   .title("Exact cycle counts and Gregorian century exceptions").fit_panels()
   .save_svg("build/doomsday_cycle.svg");
}
```
