> Negative years, extreme inputs and explicit rejection

All years use astronomical numbering: year zero is 1 BCE, year −1 is
2 BCE. Gregorian rules apply proleptically, without a Julian/Gregorian
adoption switch. Thus these functions do not reconstruct every
historically used local civil calendar. No timezone or time-of-day input
is inferred. The supported year domain is the full int range.

For year −1, floor division gives century −1 and within-century year
99. Its anchor is Wednesday (3), and (3+99+24) mod 7=0, Sunday.
Truncation toward zero would instead give century zero and remainder −1,
which breaks the rule. Year −100 exposes the old implementation clearly:
its old doomsday result is −3, outside the weekday range. The corrected
floor-based result is three, Wednesday.

The old unchecked month array can index past its end, and an arbitrary
day can produce invalid arithmetic before reduction. The canonical date
API now validates month lengths, leap days and the day range before
reference lookup or subtraction. It rejects January 0, April 31 and
February 29 in 1900 rather than silently normalizing them. The name
helper separately preserves its useful modulo-seven normalization for
arbitrary signed weekday codes. Fixed-size traces and anchor arrays need
no optional history budget and retain no references into caller storage.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/doomsday_signed.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| astronomical year | century | year within century | January 1 | year Doomsday |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(int y:{std::numeric_limits<int>::min(),-400,-100,-1,0,1,std::numeric_limits<int>::max()}) {
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::doomsday_year_trace(y);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",y,t.century,t.year_in_century,dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(dm::algorithms::doomsday_weekday(y,1,1)),dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(t.doomsday));
  }
  int rejected=0;
  for(const auto date:std::vector<std::array<int,3>>{{2025,13,1},{2025,1,0},{2025,4,31},{1900,2,29},{2025,1,std::numeric_limits<int>::min()}}) {
    try {(void)dm::algorithms::doomsday_weekday(date[0],date[1],date[2]);}
    catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {++rejected;}
  }
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::doomsday_year_trace(-1);
  std::print("\nExpected invalid-date rejections {}/5; name(-1)={}; corrected Doomsday(-100)={}.\n",rejected,dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(-1),dm::algorithms::doomsday_of_year(-100));
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(80,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=4;box.height=1.2;box.font_size=24;
  d.node("year",{0,1},"Astronomical year −1\n2 BCE",box)
   .node("split",{6.5,1},std::format("century {}; r = {}\n−1 = 100(−1) + 99",t.century,t.year_in_century),box)
   .node("weekday",{3.25,-1.4},std::format("({}+{}+{}) mod 7 = {}\n{}",t.century_anchor,t.year_in_century,t.leap_quarters,t.doomsday,dm::algorithms::doomsday_day_name(t.doomsday)),box)
   .edge("year","split","floor division")
   .edge("split","weekday")
   .text({3.25,2.4},"Negative centuries still have a remainder from 0 to 99",25)
   .save_svg("build/doomsday_signed.svg");
}
```
