\image(figures/doomsday_signed.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Floor division keeps negative years inside the same century/remainder rule. Five invalid dates are rejected before indexing or offset arithmetic; extreme int years remain supported.)
\alttext(A native diagram decomposes astronomical year minus one into century minus one and remainder 99, then obtains Sunday from anchor three plus 99 plus 24.)

> Verification and scope

Native tests check 730,485 dates against an independent sequential-day
oracle across five complete 400-year cycles. Additional std::chrono
comparisons cover representative negative, zero and positive years;
10,000 full-range int-year samples use equivalent dates reduced modulo
400 within chrono's supported year range. Monthly anchors, century/leap
boundaries, direct/dozen arithmetic identities, signed offsets, arbitrary
name codes, complete invalid-date rejection and owned copies are checked.
All original event answers, eight anchor dates, twelve month-start
comparisons and forty annual-anchor points are retained.

The method computes a weekday in a specified calendar. It does not infer
local historical adoption, timestamps, holiday calendars or daily
observations from a monthly dataset. With those conventions explicit,
one annual anchor and one monthly difference provide a constant-work,
hand-checkable weekday calculation.

> References

S. W. Graham's [The Doomsday Rule (1995 lecture notes)](https://people.se.cmich.edu/graha1sw/pub/doomsday/Doomsday.pdf)
explains Conway's century/year/reference decomposition and the dozen shortcut.
This implementation uses valid January 3/4 and March 14 references,
congruent to the notes' alternate January/March conventions, and extends
the arithmetic with floor-based astronomical years and complete validation.
The [US Naval Observatory's Gregorian leap-year rule](https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/leap_years)
specifies the century exceptions. The 400-year period, ordinal checks and
reported frequencies are derived and measured in the executed programs here.

